WEBVTT

00:00:00.795 --> 00:00:02.666
Oxford bookworms play scripts.

00:00:02.726 --> 00:00:04.418
Stage one.

00:00:04.478 --> 00:00:06.229
Sherlock Holmes.

00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:07.650
Two plays.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.382
By to Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:13.905
Text Adaptation by John Escott.

00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:18.178
Performed by Oxford University Dramatic Society.

00:00:18.238 --> 00:00:22.542
Copyright Oxford University Press 2000.

00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:34.293
The Red-Headed League.

00:00:34.353 --> 00:00:45.194
Scene one.

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:47.594
An interesting advertisement.

00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:50.145
The inside of Wilson's shop.

00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:58.158
It's a little shop with a lot of clocks, pictures, watches, etc. Wilson is writing at a table.

00:00:58.218 --> 00:01:05.914
Vincent Spalding is looking at the morning chronicle newspaper.

00:01:05.974 --> 00:01:11.429
This is interesting.

00:01:11.489 --> 00:01:13.951
What is this advertisement?

00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:18.755
It is interesting for you, Mr. Wilson, because you have red hair.

00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:19.896
Go on.

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:23.459
The red-headed league want to find someone new.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:25.450
Do you know about them?

00:01:25.511 --> 00:01:26.762
Listen.

00:01:26.822 --> 00:01:36.310
To all red-headed men, 21 years old or more, the red-headed league can now take a new man.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:40.841
We pay £4 a week to do easy work.

00:01:40.901 --> 00:01:49.943
Come to the league offices at seven popes caught Fleet Street London on Monday at 11 o'clock.

00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:52.794
You can get £200 a year.

00:01:52.854 --> 00:01:54.074
Really?

00:01:54.134 --> 00:01:55.784
Tell me about it.

00:01:55.844 --> 00:02:00.121
A rich American with red hair began the league.

00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:08.270
When he died, he gave a lot of his money to the Red-Headed League to give easy work to men with red hair.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:12.152
It's very good wages for only a little work.

00:02:12.212 --> 00:02:15.204
Why don't you go along to Pope's Court?

00:02:15.264 --> 00:02:18.265
But millions of red-headed men walked out.

00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:19.546
Millions?

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.706
You need to live in London.

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:22.667
But don't know.

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:26.428
You must have very red hair.

00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:30.030
You have very red hair, Mr. Wilson.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.911
Monday 11 o'clock.

00:02:32.971 --> 00:02:37.254
It's Monday today and it's nearly 11 o'clock.

00:02:37.314 --> 00:02:40.936
Come on!

00:02:40.996 --> 00:02:44.898
Seemed to.

00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:47.478
Some easy work for Wilson.

00:02:47.538 --> 00:02:50.240
The office at Pope's Court.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.042
A room with only a table and two chairs.

00:02:54.102 --> 00:03:00.476
Duncan Ross, a man with very red hair, is sitting behind the table.

00:03:00.536 --> 00:03:05.190
We can hear the noise of a lot of people out in the street.

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:09.232
That?

00:03:09.291 --> 00:03:17.556
This is Mr. Jabez Wilson, and he wants to be in the league.

00:03:17.616 --> 00:03:20.688
And he has a good head of hair.

00:03:20.748 --> 00:03:23.940
I can't remember when I saw a better head.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:24.941
Ah!

00:03:25.001 --> 00:03:25.982
Stop it!

00:03:26.042 --> 00:03:28.544
I'm sorry, but we must be careful.

00:03:28.604 --> 00:03:30.965
Of course, it is your hair.

00:03:31.025 --> 00:03:32.686
I can see that now.

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:34.978
Please sit down.

00:03:35.038 --> 00:03:37.990
My name is Duncan Ross.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.712
Have you got a wife, Mr Wilson?

00:03:40.772 --> 00:03:42.162
And children?

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:43.073
No.

00:03:43.132 --> 00:03:45.524
I have no wife and no family.

00:03:45.584 --> 00:03:46.985
Oh, dear.

00:03:47.045 --> 00:03:51.538
Some of the men in our league have lots of red-headed children.

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.019
We like that.

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:54.920
I'm sorry, but I...

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:55.781
It's all right.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.706
You have that wonderful head of hair.

00:03:58.766 --> 00:04:00.681
When can you begin work here?

00:04:00.741 --> 00:04:02.443
It's not easy.

00:04:02.502 --> 00:04:07.426
I have a shop and I can stay in the shop Mr. Wilson.

00:04:07.486 --> 00:04:09.708
What hours must I work?

00:04:09.768 --> 00:04:11.320
Ten o'clock to two o'clock.

00:04:11.379 --> 00:04:12.411
And the wages?

00:04:12.471 --> 00:04:13.672
Four pounds a week.

00:04:13.731 --> 00:04:15.113
What must I do?

00:04:15.173 --> 00:04:18.906
You must work here in the office all the time.

00:04:18.966 --> 00:04:21.438
That is very important.

00:04:21.498 --> 00:04:25.613
You cannot go out between the hours of ten o'clock and two o'clock.

00:04:25.673 --> 00:04:26.776
And the work?

00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:29.518
You must copy from this large book.

00:04:29.578 --> 00:04:32.520
And you must bring paper and a pen.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.332
Can you begin tomorrow?

00:04:34.392 --> 00:04:36.453
Yes, I can.

00:04:36.513 --> 00:04:43.178
Then goodbye, Mr. Java is Wilson.

00:04:43.238 --> 00:04:48.732
Scene three.

00:04:48.792 --> 00:04:51.715
Wilson tells his story.

00:04:51.774 --> 00:04:54.409
Sherlock Holmes' room.

00:04:54.469 --> 00:04:57.473
Holmes and Wilson are sitting in chairs.

00:04:57.533 --> 00:04:59.794
Holmes is smoking his pipe.

00:04:59.854 --> 00:05:03.894
Wilson has the morning chronicle in his hands.

00:05:03.954 --> 00:05:07.256
Most interesting, Mr. Wilson.

00:05:07.316 --> 00:05:08.826
Most interesting.

00:05:08.886 --> 00:05:10.076
Holmes?

00:05:10.136 --> 00:05:11.037
I'm sorry.

00:05:11.057 --> 00:05:13.787
Come in, my dear Watson.

00:05:13.847 --> 00:05:16.448
This is Mr. Jabez Wilson.

00:05:16.508 --> 00:05:19.380
He is telling me a very strange story.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.644
This is Dr. Watson.

00:05:21.703 --> 00:05:23.135
He's a good friend.

00:05:23.195 --> 00:05:25.918
Give him the advertisement to read.

00:05:25.977 --> 00:05:29.350
He does a lot of writing, Watson, do you see?

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.374
Oh, how did he know that, Mr. Holmes?

00:05:33.434 --> 00:05:36.868
The right arm of your coat is different from the left.

00:05:36.927 --> 00:05:39.940
Your arm is on the table when you write.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.597
Yes, that's right.

00:05:42.657 --> 00:05:45.294
It's very strange, Holmes.

00:05:45.354 --> 00:05:47.175
It is strange, isn't it?

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:49.907
Do you see the day and month of the newspaper?

00:05:49.967 --> 00:05:56.692
It's the morning chronicle of the 27th of April, 1890, two months ago.

00:05:56.752 --> 00:05:58.063
Now, Mr. Wilson.

00:05:58.123 --> 00:05:59.534
Tell us about your shop.

00:05:59.594 --> 00:06:03.216
It's in Saxe-Cobog Square, Mr. Holmes.

00:06:03.276 --> 00:06:07.120
It's a little shop, and it doesn't make much money.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.151
Does somebody help you in the shop?

00:06:09.211 --> 00:06:10.142
Yes.

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:11.684
A young man.

00:06:11.744 --> 00:06:16.380
Well, he's not very young, but he works for half wages.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.642
Does he?

00:06:17.702 --> 00:06:18.974
What's his name?

00:06:19.034 --> 00:06:21.047
Vincent Spalding.

00:06:21.106 --> 00:06:23.500
He's very good at his work.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.803
He likes to take photographs when he's not working.

00:06:26.863 --> 00:06:29.484
and he makes the pictures in my dark cellar.

00:06:29.544 --> 00:06:32.015
But he works well when he's in the shop.

00:06:32.075 --> 00:06:35.077
How did you first see the newspaper advertisement?

00:06:35.137 --> 00:06:36.248
Tell us that.

00:06:36.307 --> 00:06:37.848
Spolding so it.

00:06:37.908 --> 00:06:38.779
Eight weeks ago.

00:06:38.799 --> 00:06:43.342
And we went to Pope's Court and saw Mr Duncan Ross.

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:45.984
I told you about the visit a few minutes ago.

00:06:46.024 --> 00:06:46.834
Yes, yes.

00:06:46.894 --> 00:06:48.926
And I can tell what's in about it later.

00:06:48.986 --> 00:06:50.326
What happened next?

00:06:50.386 --> 00:06:53.788
Well, I was very pleased, Mr Holmes.

00:06:53.848 --> 00:06:56.350
I went home feeling very happy.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.212
But that evening I began to think again.

00:06:59.272 --> 00:07:04.035
Topping from a book is strange work for a man to do, I thought.

00:07:04.095 --> 00:07:05.756
Very strange, yes.

00:07:05.816 --> 00:07:10.386
But the next morning I went to Pope's court with my pen and some paper.

00:07:10.446 --> 00:07:15.744
Mr Ross was there, but he soon went away and I began my work.

00:07:15.804 --> 00:07:26.078
Well, I went in every morning at 10 o'clock and went home at two and on Saturday Ross was in the office again and gave me my four pounds.

00:07:26.138 --> 00:07:27.778
How long did you work there?

00:07:27.838 --> 00:07:35.570
Eight weeks, but today I went to the office at 10 o'clock and the door was locked.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.396
This was on the door.

00:07:38.456 --> 00:07:44.254
The red-headed league is finished, June the 23rd, 1890.

00:07:44.314 --> 00:07:46.504
Then what did you do?

00:07:46.564 --> 00:07:53.709
I went to the office in the next building, and I asked the man, what happened to the red-headed league?

00:07:53.769 --> 00:07:55.170
Who are they?

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:58.892
He asked, who is the man at number seven?

00:07:58.952 --> 00:08:02.234
I asked, the man with the red hair.

00:08:02.294 --> 00:08:06.159
He said, his name is William Morris.

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:08.703
He moved out yesterday.

00:08:08.762 --> 00:08:14.054
He has new offices at 17 King Edward Street.

00:08:14.114 --> 00:08:18.394
Well, I went to King Edward Street, Mr. Holmes.

00:08:18.454 --> 00:08:22.225
You did not find Mr. William Morris or Mr. Duncan Ross.

00:08:22.285 --> 00:08:23.105
Am I right?

00:08:23.165 --> 00:08:24.546
You are.

00:08:24.606 --> 00:08:26.396
What did you do then?

00:08:26.456 --> 00:08:29.176
I went home to Saxe-Coburg Square.

00:08:29.236 --> 00:08:31.527
What does Spalding say about all this?

00:08:31.587 --> 00:08:34.048
He says, wait for a letter.

00:08:34.107 --> 00:08:36.222
You're going to hear something.

00:08:36.282 --> 00:08:37.979
What do you think?

00:08:38.038 --> 00:08:40.438
I need your help, I think.

00:08:40.498 --> 00:08:42.209
You are right, Mr. Wilson.

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:43.750
I'm happy to help you.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:47.262
But first, I wanted to ask you one or two questions.

00:08:47.322 --> 00:08:50.663
Mr. Spawling was the first to see the advertisement, yes?

00:08:50.723 --> 00:08:51.604
Yes.

00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:53.915
When did he begin to work for you?

00:08:53.975 --> 00:08:56.156
About three months ago.

00:08:56.216 --> 00:08:57.517
How did he come?

00:08:57.577 --> 00:09:00.508
He answered an advertisement in the newspaper.

00:09:00.568 --> 00:09:02.900
Was he the only man to answer it?

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:03.880
No.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.031
Twelve people answered it.

00:09:06.091 --> 00:09:07.780
Why did you take him?

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.240
because he was cheap.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:13.802
He is happy to work for half the usual wages.

00:09:13.862 --> 00:09:14.753
Yes.

00:09:14.813 --> 00:09:17.585
Tell me about him this Vincent Spalding.

00:09:17.645 --> 00:09:21.448
He's small and a good, quick worker.

00:09:21.508 --> 00:09:24.310
He's about 30 years old, I think.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.671
Where is he now?

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:27.232
He's at the shop.

00:09:27.292 --> 00:09:29.204
That is all, Mr Wilson.

00:09:29.264 --> 00:09:30.735
Today is Saturday.

00:09:30.795 --> 00:09:33.307
I'm going to have an answer for you before Monday.

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:37.722
Thank you, Mr. Holmes.

00:09:37.782 --> 00:09:42.315
We must work quickly, Watson.

00:09:42.376 --> 00:09:43.696
Put on your hat.

00:09:43.756 --> 00:09:48.066
We are going to Saxe-Koburg Square.

00:09:48.126 --> 00:09:51.348
Scene four.

00:09:51.408 --> 00:09:53.978
Homes meet spalding.

00:09:54.038 --> 00:09:57.729
Outside the shop in Saxe-Koburg Square.

00:09:57.789 --> 00:10:01.040
Homes is walking up and down.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:06.031
Now and then he hits the ground outside the shop with his walking stick.

00:10:06.091 --> 00:10:09.344
Then he hits the door of the shop.

00:10:09.404 --> 00:10:13.278
Spalding opens the shop door and looks out.

00:10:13.338 --> 00:10:24.306
The legs of his trousers are dirty.

00:10:24.366 --> 00:10:33.934
Can I help you?

00:10:33.994 --> 00:10:34.604
Yes.

00:10:34.664 --> 00:10:36.496
How can I get to the strand?

00:10:36.556 --> 00:10:39.538
Third on the right and fourth on the left.

00:10:39.598 --> 00:10:42.278
He's a clever young man, Watson.

00:10:42.338 --> 00:10:43.548
He is?

00:10:43.608 --> 00:10:46.059
Why did you ask about the Strand?

00:10:46.119 --> 00:10:48.230
You know London very well.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.200
You wanted to see him.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.510
Not him.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:52.831
His trousers.

00:10:52.891 --> 00:10:54.352
His trousers?

00:10:54.412 --> 00:10:56.132
I don't understand.

00:10:56.192 --> 00:10:59.353
And why did you hit the ground with your stick?

00:10:59.413 --> 00:11:02.764
My dear doctor, this is not the time for talking.

00:11:02.824 --> 00:11:06.145
I must go and look at the roads behind Sakskoburg Square.

00:11:06.205 --> 00:11:07.985
Are you going to your work?

00:11:08.045 --> 00:11:08.765
Yes.

00:11:08.825 --> 00:11:10.536
I must go to the hospital.

00:11:10.596 --> 00:11:12.496
I have things to do too.

00:11:12.556 --> 00:11:14.487
But I want your help tonight.

00:11:14.547 --> 00:11:17.197
Come at ten o'clock and watch them.

00:11:17.257 --> 00:11:18.498
Yes, Holmes.

00:11:18.558 --> 00:11:22.258
Bring your gun.

00:11:22.318 --> 00:11:25.810
Scene five.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.888
To catch a thief.

00:11:27.948 --> 00:11:30.288
Sherlock Holmes' room.

00:11:30.348 --> 00:11:35.350
Holmes, Jones, and Maryweather are talking quietly.

00:11:35.411 --> 00:11:37.732
Watson comes in.

00:11:37.792 --> 00:11:43.485
Watson, you know Mr. Jones of Scotland Yard, and this is Mr. Maryweather.

00:11:43.545 --> 00:11:45.276
They're coming with us tonight.

00:11:45.336 --> 00:11:46.336
Hello, Doctor.

00:11:46.397 --> 00:11:47.958
We meet again.

00:11:48.018 --> 00:11:51.250
I'm here because I want to catch John Clay.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:56.727
He's a killer and a thief, one of the worst men in London.

00:11:56.787 --> 00:12:01.424
It isn't going to be easy to catch him, because he's very clever.

00:12:01.484 --> 00:12:03.014
It's after ten o'clock.

00:12:03.074 --> 00:12:06.155
We must go.

00:12:06.215 --> 00:12:09.346
Scene six.

00:12:09.406 --> 00:12:11.627
Watching and waiting.

00:12:11.687 --> 00:12:14.738
Inside the bank strongroom.

00:12:14.798 --> 00:12:17.409
There are boxes on the floor.

00:12:17.469 --> 00:12:19.540
It is dark.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.662
The door opens and homes comes in, carrying a light.

00:12:24.722 --> 00:12:29.053
Watson, Jones and Maryweather come after him.

00:12:29.113 --> 00:12:32.364
Holmes looks carefully at the floor.

00:12:32.424 --> 00:12:41.206
Maryweather sits on a box and hits the floor with his foot.

00:12:41.266 --> 00:12:47.697
How do you believe?

00:12:47.757 --> 00:12:48.707
No noise.

00:12:48.767 --> 00:12:50.148
Perhaps they can hear us.

00:12:50.208 --> 00:12:51.058
How long before?

00:12:51.118 --> 00:12:51.988
An hour.

00:12:52.048 --> 00:12:56.909
They're going to wait for Mr. Wilson to go to bed, then work quickly, I think.

00:12:56.969 --> 00:12:59.430
We're in the strongroom of the first bank.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.610
Did you know that?

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.161
Mr. Merryweather works for the bank.

00:13:03.221 --> 00:13:05.512
Tell Watson, Mr. Merryweather.

00:13:05.572 --> 00:13:07.462
What do the thieves want?

00:13:07.522 --> 00:13:09.213
Our gold.

00:13:09.273 --> 00:13:11.864
It's in all these boxes.

00:13:11.924 --> 00:13:14.515
Lots and lots of it.

00:13:14.575 --> 00:13:15.656
Gold?

00:13:15.716 --> 00:13:17.546
We must wait in the dark.

00:13:17.606 --> 00:13:19.764
Get behind the boxes, everyone.

00:13:19.824 --> 00:13:23.063
Things are going to happen in the next hour, I think.

00:13:23.123 --> 00:13:30.387
When I hear or see anything strange, I'm going to come out quickly with a light, and then you must help me.

00:13:30.448 --> 00:13:33.130
Be ready with your gun, Watson.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:34.811
Is everything ready?

00:13:34.871 --> 00:13:36.452
Upstairs, Mr Jones?

00:13:36.512 --> 00:13:39.364
My men are waiting at the front door of the bank, Holmes.

00:13:39.424 --> 00:13:40.935
They can't get out there.

00:13:40.995 --> 00:13:41.805
Good.

00:13:41.866 --> 00:13:45.638
Now we must be quiet and wait.

00:13:45.698 --> 00:13:48.513
It's 1115, Holmes.

00:13:48.573 --> 00:13:53.950
Once.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:59.572
Now, have you got?

00:13:59.632 --> 00:14:00.673
What?

00:14:00.733 --> 00:14:03.525
You can't get away, John Clay.

00:14:03.585 --> 00:14:04.725
No, but my friend.

00:14:04.785 --> 00:14:08.397
There are three men waiting for him at the front door of the bank.

00:14:08.457 --> 00:14:11.199
You think of everything, Mr. Holmes.

00:14:11.259 --> 00:14:13.400
You're very clever.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:15.871
Your red-headed league was clever, too.

00:14:15.931 --> 00:14:21.632
But I am pleased to catch the famous John Clay.

00:14:21.692 --> 00:14:25.392
Scene seven.

00:14:25.452 --> 00:14:27.744
Holmes and Watson.

00:14:27.804 --> 00:14:30.176
Sherlock Holmes' room.

00:14:30.236 --> 00:14:33.077
Holmes and Watson are sitting in chairs.

00:14:33.137 --> 00:14:35.988
Holmes is smoking his pipe.

00:14:36.048 --> 00:14:41.222
They needed the red-headed league to get Mr. Wilson out of his shop.

00:14:41.282 --> 00:14:42.962
Do you understand that?

00:14:43.022 --> 00:14:44.924
Yes, I think.

00:14:44.983 --> 00:14:46.794
Um, yes.

00:14:46.854 --> 00:14:51.557
They wanted Wilson away from the shop for some hours every day.

00:14:51.617 --> 00:14:53.452
But how could they do it?

00:14:53.512 --> 00:14:59.388
Then Clay saw the color of Ross's hair and thought of a red-headed leak.

00:14:59.449 --> 00:15:05.842
When Wilson answered their newspaper advertisement, he went to work in their office every day.

00:15:05.902 --> 00:15:09.014
Then they had time to make their towel.

00:15:09.074 --> 00:15:10.504
Very clever.

00:15:10.564 --> 00:15:15.267
So, Spalding is John Clay, the famous thief.

00:15:15.326 --> 00:15:17.538
When did you first know that?

00:15:17.598 --> 00:15:20.899
Spalding was happy to work for half wages.

00:15:20.959 --> 00:15:22.436
Why, I thought.

00:15:22.496 --> 00:15:25.223
Spalding often went down to the cellar.

00:15:25.283 --> 00:15:28.214
What's he doing down there, I thought?

00:15:28.274 --> 00:15:32.195
Suddenly, I thought about tunnels.

00:15:32.256 --> 00:15:35.297
Is Spalding making a tunnel, I thought?

00:15:35.357 --> 00:15:37.198
To another building?

00:15:37.258 --> 00:15:42.820
Watson, you saw me hitting the ground with my stick outside the shop.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.030
Yes.

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:45.991
Now I understand.

00:15:46.051 --> 00:15:48.992
Was the cellar in front of the shop?

00:15:49.052 --> 00:15:50.732
You wanted to know that.

00:15:50.792 --> 00:15:52.409
Yes, I did.

00:15:52.469 --> 00:15:53.916
And it wasn't.

00:15:53.976 --> 00:15:56.658
The cellar was behind the shop.

00:15:56.718 --> 00:15:59.400
Then I saw the man spalding.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.822
Did you see his trousers?

00:16:01.882 --> 00:16:04.734
Yes, I saw them.

00:16:04.794 --> 00:16:06.525
They were dirty Watson.

00:16:06.585 --> 00:16:07.526
Why?

00:16:07.586 --> 00:16:10.458
Because making a tunnel is dirty work.

00:16:10.518 --> 00:16:13.310
Very clever homes.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:17.433
Then I went into the next street at the back of the shop.

00:16:17.493 --> 00:16:19.342
And what did I see?

00:16:19.402 --> 00:16:21.190
The first bank.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.851
The bank, yes, of course.

00:16:23.911 --> 00:16:27.122
Why is that young man making a tunnel, I thought?

00:16:27.182 --> 00:16:30.063
To get into the bank's strongroom.

00:16:30.123 --> 00:16:32.394
But why tonight?

00:16:32.454 --> 00:16:34.385
How did you know?

00:16:34.445 --> 00:16:37.806
Because they closed the offices of the red-headed league.

00:16:37.866 --> 00:16:40.306
The tunnel must be ready, I thought.

00:16:40.366 --> 00:16:42.477
And Saturday is a good day.

00:16:42.537 --> 00:16:45.258
The bank does not open on Sunday.

00:16:45.318 --> 00:16:48.921
Two days for the thieves to get away.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.992
But they didn't get away, Holmes.

00:16:52.052 --> 00:16:54.024
You're very clever.

00:16:54.084 --> 00:17:02.639
That's very true, Watson.

00:17:02.699 --> 00:17:15.698
The three students.

00:17:15.759 --> 00:17:34.830
Scene 1 Hilton Somes' Story Sherlock Holmes is sitting at a table, writing.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:36.712
It is evening.

00:17:36.772 --> 00:17:42.916
Watson comes in with Hilton Somes.

00:17:42.976 --> 00:17:47.890
Who was at the door, Watson?

00:17:47.951 --> 00:17:51.223
This is Mr Hilton's home's home.

00:17:51.283 --> 00:17:53.545
He teaches at St Luke's College.

00:17:53.605 --> 00:17:56.618
Can you give me some of your time, Mr Holmes?

00:17:56.678 --> 00:18:01.092
Today something happened at the college and I was very worried.

00:18:01.152 --> 00:18:07.928
Then I thought the famous detective Sherlock Holmes is visiting our town.

00:18:07.988 --> 00:18:09.701
Perhaps he can help.

00:18:09.761 --> 00:18:10.363
And I...

00:18:10.383 --> 00:18:12.776
I have a lot of work to do, Mr. Soams.

00:18:12.836 --> 00:18:13.766
Perhaps the...

00:18:13.826 --> 00:18:14.958
No, no.

00:18:15.018 --> 00:18:17.040
I can't go to the police.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:20.162
Well, tell me your story, Mr. Soams.

00:18:20.222 --> 00:18:27.169
Tomorrow is the first day of an important examination, and I'm one of the examiners.

00:18:27.229 --> 00:18:32.814
Today, at three o'clock, the three exam papers arrived for me to read.

00:18:32.874 --> 00:18:38.458
Of course, every student would like to see these papers before the exam.

00:18:38.518 --> 00:18:39.719
Of course.

00:18:39.779 --> 00:18:42.390
We are always very careful with them.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:49.716
I began to read them, but at 4.30pm, I went out to have tea in a friend's rooms.

00:18:49.776 --> 00:18:51.297
Where did you leave the paper?

00:18:51.357 --> 00:18:53.438
They were on my writing table.

00:18:53.498 --> 00:18:57.141
I locked my door and was away for an hour.

00:18:57.201 --> 00:19:01.612
When I arrived home, I saw a key in my door.

00:19:01.672 --> 00:19:04.893
Is it my key, I thought?

00:19:04.953 --> 00:19:07.754
No, that's in my pocket.

00:19:07.814 --> 00:19:10.884
Ah, it's Banister's key.

00:19:10.944 --> 00:19:13.015
Banister is my servant.

00:19:13.075 --> 00:19:15.846
Why did he go to your room this afternoon?

00:19:15.906 --> 00:19:19.046
He brings my tea in every afternoon.

00:19:19.106 --> 00:19:21.087
But you were at your friend's rooms.

00:19:21.147 --> 00:19:22.427
Did he forget that?

00:19:22.487 --> 00:19:26.122
Yes, and so his key was in my door.

00:19:26.183 --> 00:19:30.854
He's usually careful, but tell me about the examination paper.

00:19:30.914 --> 00:19:33.738
Where were they when you went back to your room?

00:19:33.798 --> 00:19:37.968
One was on a small table next to the window.

00:19:38.028 --> 00:19:42.606
One was on the floor, and one on my writing table.

00:19:42.666 --> 00:19:48.921
The first paper on the floor, the second in the window, and the third on the writing table.

00:19:48.981 --> 00:19:49.902
Yes.

00:19:49.962 --> 00:19:51.472
How did you know that?

00:19:51.532 --> 00:19:52.203
Please.

00:19:52.263 --> 00:19:55.625
Tell us more of your very interesting story, Mr. Stone.

00:19:55.686 --> 00:20:00.208
First, I thought, did Banister look at the papers?

00:20:00.268 --> 00:20:04.960
So I asked him, but he said, no, I did not.

00:20:05.019 --> 00:20:08.319
And he is an honest man, Mr. Holmes.

00:20:08.379 --> 00:20:10.170
What happened next?

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:15.594
Banister suddenly began to feel ill when I told him about the papers.

00:20:15.654 --> 00:20:19.416
He sat down on a chair and I gave him a strong drink.

00:20:19.476 --> 00:20:22.929
Then I looked carefully at the table next to the window.

00:20:22.989 --> 00:20:24.690
What did you find?

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.492
Small pieces of pencil.

00:20:27.552 --> 00:20:30.094
Did somebody break and sharpen a pencil?

00:20:30.154 --> 00:20:32.166
Yes, perhaps.

00:20:32.226 --> 00:20:34.097
Yes, that was it.

00:20:34.157 --> 00:20:34.850
Good.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:36.234
That helps her.

00:20:36.294 --> 00:20:38.254
Two more things.

00:20:38.315 --> 00:20:43.616
I have a new writing table, but now there's a little cut on it.

00:20:43.676 --> 00:20:47.468
And there was a piece of black mud on the table.

00:20:47.528 --> 00:20:48.769
Is that all?

00:20:48.829 --> 00:20:50.049
Yes.

00:20:50.109 --> 00:20:53.350
Please help me, Mr. Holmes.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.232
It's an interesting story, Mr. Serms.

00:20:56.292 --> 00:21:01.754
Tell me, did any student visit you in your room after the papers arrived this afternoon?

00:21:01.815 --> 00:21:06.557
Yes, Daula Tarras, a student from India.

00:21:06.617 --> 00:21:08.858
And the papers were on your writing table?

00:21:08.918 --> 00:21:11.559
Yes, but he couldn't read them.

00:21:11.619 --> 00:21:14.300
He was two or three meters away.

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:16.291
But they were exam papers.

00:21:16.351 --> 00:21:17.661
Could he see that?

00:21:17.721 --> 00:21:19.122
Perhaps.

00:21:19.182 --> 00:21:22.053
The examination papers were in your room.

00:21:22.113 --> 00:21:23.784
Did anyone know that?

00:21:23.844 --> 00:21:25.905
Did Banister know that?

00:21:25.965 --> 00:21:28.156
No, nobody.

00:21:28.216 --> 00:21:29.946
Where is Banister now?

00:21:30.007 --> 00:21:36.164
When I ran out, To find you, Mr. Holmes, he was on the chair in my room.

00:21:36.224 --> 00:21:38.226
He looked very ill.

00:21:38.286 --> 00:21:40.058
Did you leave your door open?

00:21:40.118 --> 00:21:40.988
Yes.

00:21:41.048 --> 00:21:43.931
But I locked the papers up first.

00:21:43.991 --> 00:21:45.122
Come then.

00:21:45.182 --> 00:21:49.976
We must go to your room.

00:21:50.036 --> 00:21:54.480
Scene two.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.208
Holmes visits the college.

00:21:57.269 --> 00:22:02.388
Soams, homes and Watson come into Soams's living room.

00:22:02.448 --> 00:22:07.842
Homes walks to the window and looks out.

00:22:07.902 --> 00:22:13.096
That window does not open, Mr. Holmes.

00:22:13.156 --> 00:22:16.488
Nobody can enter the room through there.

00:22:16.548 --> 00:22:19.130
Your servant isn't here now.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:21.282
Perhaps he's feeling better.

00:22:21.342 --> 00:22:23.593
Which chair did you leave him on?

00:22:23.653 --> 00:22:25.474
The chair by the window.

00:22:25.534 --> 00:22:27.963
Near this little table.

00:22:28.023 --> 00:22:31.142
Yeah, yes, of course.

00:22:31.202 --> 00:22:32.802
The man comes in.

00:22:32.862 --> 00:22:39.175
He takes the three papers from the writing table to carry them across to the window.

00:22:39.235 --> 00:22:44.808
Because then he can see you when you come across the garden to the front door so.

00:22:44.868 --> 00:22:49.449
But he didn't see me because I walked in through the back door.

00:22:49.509 --> 00:22:51.390
Ah, that helps.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:56.387
Can I see the three papers now?

00:22:56.447 --> 00:23:05.286
He carries this first paper across to the table near the window and begins to copy it.

00:23:05.346 --> 00:23:07.988
It takes him ten minutes?

00:23:08.048 --> 00:23:09.508
Fifteen, I think.

00:23:09.568 --> 00:23:10.689
All right.

00:23:10.749 --> 00:23:12.000
Fifteen.

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:18.352
Then he puts it down on the floor, gets the next paper and begins to copy that.

00:23:18.412 --> 00:23:22.295
Then he hears you coming in the back door, Mr. Somes.

00:23:22.355 --> 00:23:23.626
He must be quick.

00:23:23.686 --> 00:23:28.486
He leaves the second paper on the table next to the window and runs.

00:23:28.547 --> 00:23:29.916
Where?

00:23:29.976 --> 00:23:32.617
Did you hear him running upstairs?

00:23:32.677 --> 00:23:35.138
No, I didn't.

00:23:35.198 --> 00:23:40.662
He's writing quickly because he breaks his pencil and needs to sharpen it again.

00:23:40.722 --> 00:23:42.203
This is interesting.

00:23:42.263 --> 00:23:44.784
The pencil was long and dark blue.

00:23:44.844 --> 00:23:46.656
Look at these pieces.

00:23:46.716 --> 00:23:50.498
And do you see the gold letters N-N?

00:23:50.558 --> 00:23:53.289
It's at the end of a word.

00:23:53.349 --> 00:23:55.941
Do you know Johann Faber pencils?

00:23:56.001 --> 00:23:57.202
I do, Holmes.

00:23:57.262 --> 00:23:59.836
I write for them.

00:23:59.896 --> 00:24:04.720
This is the piece of black mud.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.241
I can see the cut.

00:24:07.301 --> 00:24:09.312
What is that door?

00:24:09.372 --> 00:24:11.803
It opens into my bedroom.

00:24:11.863 --> 00:24:15.424
Did you go into your bedroom before you ran out to find me?

00:24:15.484 --> 00:24:18.465
No.

00:24:18.525 --> 00:24:24.078
A man can hide behind this curtain.

00:24:24.138 --> 00:24:26.098
What's this?

00:24:26.158 --> 00:24:27.929
It's a mud again.

00:24:27.989 --> 00:24:31.180
Your visitor was in this room, Mr. Somes.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:32.962
Why?

00:24:33.022 --> 00:24:34.482
Think for a minute.

00:24:34.542 --> 00:24:36.344
He hears you coming.

00:24:36.404 --> 00:24:38.054
What can he do?

00:24:38.114 --> 00:24:39.745
He must hide.

00:24:39.805 --> 00:24:44.258
So he runs into your bedroom and hides behind this curtain.

00:24:44.318 --> 00:24:45.098
Oh.

00:24:45.158 --> 00:24:50.331
Then he was in here, when Vanister and I were in the next room.

00:24:50.391 --> 00:24:51.082
Yes.

00:24:51.142 --> 00:24:52.432
That's right.

00:24:52.492 --> 00:24:56.784
Tell me, how many students live upstairs and go past your door?

00:24:56.845 --> 00:24:58.045
Three.

00:24:58.105 --> 00:25:00.706
All taking this examination.

00:25:00.767 --> 00:25:01.876
Who are they?

00:25:01.936 --> 00:25:05.038
There is Gilchrist.

00:25:05.098 --> 00:25:07.950
He plays football and likes running.

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:11.482
His father was Sir Jabez, Gilchrist.

00:25:11.542 --> 00:25:15.584
He was a rich man, but he gambled his money away.

00:25:15.644 --> 00:25:20.107
Young Gilchrist hasn't much money, but he works hard.

00:25:20.167 --> 00:25:21.838
And the Indian student?

00:25:21.898 --> 00:25:22.799
Yes.

00:25:22.859 --> 00:25:28.000
Dalat Ras is a quiet young man, but he works well.

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:38.739
Then there is Miles McLaren, a very clever young man, but he doesn't study much, and he's worried about this examination.

00:25:38.799 --> 00:25:41.160
So he would like to see the papers first?

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:44.790
Well, yes, perhaps.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:49.471
Can we speak to Banister yourself?

00:25:49.532 --> 00:25:55.622
Scene three, some questions for Banister.

00:25:55.683 --> 00:25:58.116
Insomes is living room.

00:25:58.176 --> 00:26:02.962
Holmes, Watson and soams are sitting in chairs.

00:26:03.022 --> 00:26:06.284
Holmes is sitting at the writing table.

00:26:06.344 --> 00:26:10.497
Banister is standing in front of him.

00:26:10.557 --> 00:26:15.331
Your key was in the door of this room today, Banister.

00:26:15.391 --> 00:26:16.261
Is that right?

00:26:16.322 --> 00:26:17.762
Yes, sir.

00:26:17.822 --> 00:26:19.844
I don't usually leave it there.

00:26:19.904 --> 00:26:22.816
But tell me, when did you come into the room?

00:26:22.876 --> 00:26:25.608
About 4.30pm, sir.

00:26:25.668 --> 00:26:29.458
With Mr. Services T, But he wasn't here.

00:26:29.518 --> 00:26:31.910
Did you look at the papers on the table?

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:33.350
No, sir.

00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:35.832
Why did you leave the key in the door?

00:26:35.892 --> 00:26:39.194
The key things were in my hands.

00:26:39.254 --> 00:26:42.747
I thought, I can come back for the key.

00:26:42.807 --> 00:26:44.828
Did you forget to come back?

00:26:44.888 --> 00:26:46.410
Yes, sir.

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:48.903
So the room was open all the time?

00:26:48.963 --> 00:26:51.806
and somebody in the room could get in or out?

00:26:51.866 --> 00:26:54.076
Yes, yes, sir.

00:26:54.136 --> 00:27:01.688
When Mr. Somes talked to you about the papers you began to feel ill, so you sat down on the chair over there.

00:27:01.748 --> 00:27:04.449
Why did you go past the other chairs?

00:27:04.509 --> 00:27:05.509
I...

00:27:05.569 --> 00:27:07.790
I don't know, sir.

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:10.710
Did you stay here when Mr. Somes ran out?

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:13.421
Only for a minute or two.

00:27:13.481 --> 00:27:16.565
Then I locked the door and went to my room.

00:27:16.625 --> 00:27:20.781
Mr. Soams, I would like to visit the three young gentlemen.

00:27:20.801 --> 00:27:21.602
Is that all right?

00:27:21.662 --> 00:27:23.193
Yes, of course.

00:27:23.253 --> 00:27:27.106
These rooms are very old and very interesting.

00:27:27.166 --> 00:27:32.070
Visitors often go to look at them.

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:35.814
Scene four.

00:27:35.874 --> 00:27:37.945
Gilchrist.

00:27:38.005 --> 00:27:44.110
Homes, soams and Watson are outside the door of Gilchrist's room.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:46.872
They are looking at the doorway.

00:27:46.932 --> 00:27:51.124
Remember, gentlemen, you do not have a pencil with you.

00:27:51.184 --> 00:27:53.365
What are you...

00:27:53.425 --> 00:27:54.995
Oh, it's you, Mr. Stones.

00:27:55.055 --> 00:27:59.737
Gilchrist, this is Mr. Holmes and his friend.

00:27:59.797 --> 00:28:01.968
They're looking at the building.

00:28:01.988 --> 00:28:04.289
Have you got a pencil, Watson?

00:28:04.349 --> 00:28:07.681
This is a most interesting doorway and I want to draw it.

00:28:07.741 --> 00:28:08.771
Oh, no.

00:28:08.831 --> 00:28:10.002
I'm sorry, Holmes.

00:28:10.062 --> 00:28:11.722
Have you, Mr. Gilchrist?

00:28:11.782 --> 00:28:12.663
A pencil?

00:28:12.723 --> 00:28:14.744
Oh, yes.

00:28:14.804 --> 00:28:17.588
There you are, Mr. Holmes.

00:28:17.648 --> 00:28:19.442
It is a beautiful building.

00:28:19.502 --> 00:28:23.092
Ah, thank you.

00:28:23.153 --> 00:28:27.524
Yes, Mr. Gilchrist.

00:28:27.584 --> 00:28:30.905
A beautiful building.

00:28:30.965 --> 00:28:34.026
Scene 5.

00:28:34.086 --> 00:28:36.136
Dalit Rass.

00:28:36.196 --> 00:28:41.498
Homes, Watson and soams are in Dalit Rass' room.

00:28:41.558 --> 00:28:45.490
The Indian is walking up and down the room.

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:47.894
He looks worried.

00:28:47.954 --> 00:28:53.581
Holmes is drawing on a piece of paper.

00:28:53.641 --> 00:28:58.914
A very interesting room, Holmes.

00:28:58.974 --> 00:29:01.346
Thank you, Mr. Ross.

00:29:01.406 --> 00:29:05.650
Are you worried about something?

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:06.730
Sorry.

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:08.162
What did you say?

00:29:08.222 --> 00:29:09.592
Worried?

00:29:09.652 --> 00:29:11.344
Oh, yes.

00:29:11.404 --> 00:29:13.644
It's the examination.

00:29:13.704 --> 00:29:14.964
Is that all?

00:29:15.024 --> 00:29:16.716
Yes, of course.

00:29:16.776 --> 00:29:21.480
What good evening, Mr. Ross?

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:24.894
Scene six.

00:29:24.954 --> 00:29:27.426
Miles McLaren.

00:29:27.486 --> 00:29:34.937
Holmes, Watson and Somes are outside the door of Miles McLaren's room.

00:29:34.997 --> 00:29:36.612
Go away.

00:29:36.672 --> 00:29:37.672
I'm busy.

00:29:37.732 --> 00:29:39.394
I can't see anybody.

00:29:39.454 --> 00:29:40.835
Go away once.

00:29:40.895 --> 00:29:42.106
Look, claren.

00:29:42.166 --> 00:29:43.256
This is mister.

00:29:43.316 --> 00:29:44.147
Go away.

00:29:44.207 --> 00:29:46.128
It's the examination tomorrow.

00:29:46.188 --> 00:29:47.389
I must study.

00:29:47.449 --> 00:29:49.000
Come, my friends.

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:50.302
It is not important.

00:29:50.362 --> 00:29:54.024
I'm sorry mister Holmes, but is he a tall young man?

00:29:54.084 --> 00:29:55.375
Tall.

00:29:55.435 --> 00:29:56.566
No.

00:29:56.626 --> 00:29:58.518
McLaren isn't tall.

00:29:58.578 --> 00:30:01.720
Gilchrist is the tallest of the three.

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:02.962
Good.

00:30:03.022 --> 00:30:04.903
And now, Mr. Somes.

00:30:04.963 --> 00:30:05.924
Goodnight.

00:30:05.984 --> 00:30:06.964
What?

00:30:07.024 --> 00:30:08.756
Are you going?

00:30:08.817 --> 00:30:12.150
But tomorrow is the examination.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:13.691
What can I do?

00:30:13.711 --> 00:30:17.824
I'm going to take the pieces of pencil and the mud with me.

00:30:17.884 --> 00:30:18.996
Don't worry.

00:30:19.056 --> 00:30:24.128
I'm going to come back tomorrow morning with the answer.

00:30:24.188 --> 00:30:27.648
Scene seven.

00:30:27.708 --> 00:30:30.219
Holmes is thinking.

00:30:30.279 --> 00:30:32.380
Holmes' room.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:36.281
He and Watson are sitting in chairs.

00:30:36.341 --> 00:30:39.162
What do you think, Watson?

00:30:39.222 --> 00:30:41.493
Who began to copy the papers?

00:30:41.553 --> 00:30:42.702
MacLaurin.

00:30:42.762 --> 00:30:47.563
He said, go away, but did he want to hide something?

00:30:47.623 --> 00:30:53.684
Or perhaps the student from India, Mr. Russ, he looked very worried.

00:30:53.744 --> 00:30:57.095
Many people are worried before an examination, Watson.

00:30:57.155 --> 00:31:01.136
It is nothing, and the pencil was no help to us.

00:31:01.196 --> 00:31:03.667
What about Soums' servant?

00:31:03.727 --> 00:31:04.875
Banister.

00:31:04.935 --> 00:31:07.665
He's an honest man, Somme says.

00:31:07.725 --> 00:31:09.376
That's true, I think.

00:31:09.436 --> 00:31:10.448
Yeah.

00:31:10.508 --> 00:31:13.760
So why did an honest man...

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.414
Oh dear, I must think.

00:31:17.474 --> 00:31:21.447
Where's my pipe, Watson?

00:31:21.507 --> 00:31:24.660
Scenate.

00:31:24.721 --> 00:31:28.293
All the answers to all the questions.

00:31:28.353 --> 00:31:30.856
Somme's room.

00:31:30.916 --> 00:31:33.204
It is morning.

00:31:33.264 --> 00:31:39.324
A very worried psalms is bringing Holmes and Watson into the room.

00:31:39.384 --> 00:31:45.416
Well, Mr. Holmes, can we have the examination later this morning?

00:31:45.476 --> 00:31:47.097
Or must I stop it?

00:31:47.157 --> 00:31:49.889
You can have the examination, Mr. Psalms.

00:31:49.949 --> 00:31:52.360
But the young man isn't going to take it.

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:53.380
You know him?

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:55.011
Yes, I do.

00:31:55.071 --> 00:31:58.112
Please call Banister.

00:31:58.172 --> 00:32:01.274
You have the answer, Holmes?

00:32:01.334 --> 00:32:03.260
Yes, my dear Watson.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:04.586
Three?

00:32:04.646 --> 00:32:07.247
There were only two pieces of mud yesterday.

00:32:07.307 --> 00:32:10.669
Where did the third piece come from?

00:32:10.729 --> 00:32:15.552
Now, Banister, I want the true story.

00:32:15.612 --> 00:32:16.893
What happened yesterday?

00:32:16.953 --> 00:32:19.124
I told you everything, sir.

00:32:19.184 --> 00:32:21.492
You have nothing more to say.

00:32:21.552 --> 00:32:23.440
Then I must tell you.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:31.002
When Mr. Sombs called you into the room yesterday, you saw something on that chair and you sat on it before Mr. Sombs could see it.

00:32:31.062 --> 00:32:32.082
No, sir.

00:32:32.142 --> 00:32:32.932
Yes.

00:32:32.992 --> 00:32:41.845
And when Mr. Sombs went out to find me, you opened the door and said, you can come out now to the man hiding behind the curtain.

00:32:41.905 --> 00:32:44.266
There was no man, sir.

00:32:44.326 --> 00:32:45.786
That isn't true.

00:32:45.846 --> 00:32:49.546
But please go and stand next to the bedroom door.

00:32:49.606 --> 00:32:57.299
Soams, please go and ask young Gilchrist to come down.

00:32:57.359 --> 00:33:05.453
Mr. Gilchrist, there are only the five of us to hear your answer to my question.

00:33:05.513 --> 00:33:08.994
Why did you copy the examination papers yesterday?

00:33:09.054 --> 00:33:11.436
No, Mr. Gilchrist, sir.

00:33:11.496 --> 00:33:13.377
I never said anything.

00:33:13.437 --> 00:33:16.288
No, but you are helping us to our baluster.

00:33:16.348 --> 00:33:20.395
Tell us, Mr. Gilchrist.

00:33:20.456 --> 00:33:25.295
You did something very wrong, Gilchrist.

00:33:25.355 --> 00:33:26.816
But you're sorry now.

00:33:26.876 --> 00:33:28.268
I can see that.

00:33:28.328 --> 00:33:30.770
I'm going to tell you the story, soums.

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:34.504
Mr. Gilchrist first saw the papers through the window.

00:33:34.564 --> 00:33:37.868
But nobody can get in through the window.

00:33:37.928 --> 00:33:38.899
That's true.

00:33:38.959 --> 00:33:43.082
But a very tall man can look through it from the garden.

00:33:43.143 --> 00:33:47.914
I could not understand this cut, but then I remembered.

00:33:47.974 --> 00:33:50.125
Gilchrist likes running.

00:33:50.186 --> 00:33:53.187
But how does that help Holmes?

00:33:53.247 --> 00:33:55.108
Think, Watson.

00:33:55.168 --> 00:33:58.330
This young man went running yesterday afternoon.

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:00.110
When he walked home...

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:02.668
His running shoes were in his hands.

00:34:02.728 --> 00:34:05.929
And running shoes have spikes, of course.

00:34:05.989 --> 00:34:06.729
Yes.

00:34:06.789 --> 00:34:09.520
Gilchrist went past this window and looked in.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:11.690
He's a tall young man, you see.

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:14.970
He saw the exam papers on the writing table.

00:34:15.031 --> 00:34:18.781
Then he went past soams his door and saw the key in it.

00:34:18.841 --> 00:34:22.682
And he went into the room and put his shoes on the writing table.

00:34:22.742 --> 00:34:24.582
And a spike cut into it.

00:34:24.642 --> 00:34:26.012
Yes, rats.

00:34:26.073 --> 00:34:29.396
What did you put on that chair near the window?

00:34:29.457 --> 00:34:31.050
My watch.

00:34:31.111 --> 00:34:35.903
He puts his watch on the chair, then begins to copy one of the papers.

00:34:35.963 --> 00:34:40.945
I'm going to see Mr. Soames when he comes back across the garden, he thinks.

00:34:41.006 --> 00:34:44.237
But you walk in through the back door, Soames.

00:34:44.297 --> 00:34:45.688
Then what?

00:34:45.748 --> 00:34:47.929
Gilchrist hears you coming.

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:53.141
Quickly takes his running shoes and runs into your bedroom to hide.

00:34:53.201 --> 00:34:56.931
One shoe leaves some black mud behind the curtain.

00:34:56.992 --> 00:34:59.291
Is this all true, mystical, Christ?

00:34:59.351 --> 00:35:02.742
I found the same black mud on your shoes.

00:35:02.802 --> 00:35:03.792
Yes, sir.

00:35:03.852 --> 00:35:04.632
It's true.

00:35:04.692 --> 00:35:06.853
Have you nothing more to say?

00:35:06.873 --> 00:35:08.154
I...

00:35:08.214 --> 00:35:11.565
I have a letter here, Mr. Soames.

00:35:11.625 --> 00:35:15.576
Early this morning, I began to write to you.

00:35:15.636 --> 00:35:21.398
It says, I'm not going to take the examination.

00:35:21.458 --> 00:35:24.949
I'm going to South Africa to work for the police there.

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:26.502
I see.

00:35:26.562 --> 00:35:29.946
You're not going to take the examination now.

00:35:30.006 --> 00:35:32.377
I'm pleased to hear that.

00:35:32.437 --> 00:35:34.368
Mr. Bannister talked to me.

00:35:34.428 --> 00:35:36.640
This is the right thing to do.

00:35:36.701 --> 00:35:38.782
I understand that now.

00:35:38.842 --> 00:35:43.976
Gilchrist was in the bedroom, Bannister, and you didn't tell Mr. Sims.

00:35:44.036 --> 00:35:47.749
When you saw Gilchrist's watch, you sat on it to hide it.

00:35:47.809 --> 00:35:48.706
Why?

00:35:48.766 --> 00:35:56.716
Well, sir, I was once a servant to Sir Jabba's Gilchrist, this young gentleman's father.

00:35:56.776 --> 00:35:57.496
Ah!

00:35:57.556 --> 00:36:05.951
When he gambled his money away, I moved to the college to be a servant, but I always remembered Sir Jabba's.

00:36:06.012 --> 00:36:11.224
He was good to me, so I did everything to help his son here.

00:36:11.284 --> 00:36:18.818
And yesterday, when Mr. Somes called me yesterday, I saw Mr. Gilchrist's watch on the chair.

00:36:18.878 --> 00:36:27.230
He looked at the examination papers, I thought, and I quickly sat down on the watch before Mr. Soames saw it.

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:29.901
Then Mr. Soames visited me.

00:36:29.961 --> 00:36:34.172
Yes, sir, and I called young Mr. Gilchrist.

00:36:34.232 --> 00:36:38.104
You looked at the exam papers, I said to him.

00:36:38.164 --> 00:36:39.984
Did you copy them?

00:36:40.044 --> 00:36:43.125
Yes, he said, I did.

00:36:43.185 --> 00:36:46.696
Then you cannot take the exam, I said.

00:36:46.756 --> 00:36:49.154
Think of your father.

00:36:49.214 --> 00:36:53.972
He was an honest man, and you must be honest, too.

00:36:53.992 --> 00:36:57.633
I wanted to stop him, Mr. Holmes.

00:36:57.693 --> 00:36:59.414
You were right, Bannister.

00:36:59.474 --> 00:37:02.265
Well, Holmes, you have your answers now.

00:37:02.325 --> 00:37:04.476
And you, young man, did wrong.

00:37:04.536 --> 00:37:06.537
But you're going to put things right.

00:37:06.597 --> 00:37:08.828
You can thank Bannister for that.

00:37:08.888 --> 00:37:10.518
I do thank him, sir.

00:37:10.578 --> 00:37:11.809
Come, Watson.

00:37:11.869 --> 00:37:13.530
Our work is finished here.

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:17.704
Yes, Holmes.

00:37:17.764 --> 00:37:20.769
So.

